1
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:02,300
Every morning,

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,740
before anyone at work opens a laptop,

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:05,780
thousands of data

4
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:07,860
pipelines have already run.

5
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:09,060
Something decided when

6
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:10,420
each one should start,

7
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:11,460
in what order,

8
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,620
and what to do when one fails.

9
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,020
In a lot of companies,

10
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,620
that something is Apache Airflow.

11
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,380
Airflow is an open-source tool

12
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,300
for orchestrating workflows.

13
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:22,660
Orchestrating just means

14
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:23,900
running the right task,

15
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,260
at the right time, in the right order.

16
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:28,900
It started at Airbnb in 2014,

17
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,780
and today it's a top-level Apache project.

18
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:32,980
You write each pipeline

19
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,220
in Python, as a DAG.

20
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,340
DAG just means directed acyclic graph.

21
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:41,580
Each box is a task: extract the orders,

22
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:42,340
transform them,

23
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:44,180
and load them into the warehouse.

24
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,020
The arrows are dependencies,

25
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:47,060
so a task only

26
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:49,940
runs after the tasks before it succeed.

27
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:51,460
Now, the architecture.

28
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:54,060
The DAG processor reads your Python files

29
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:55,500
and saves each DAG

30
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,220
to the metadata database.

31
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:58,700
The scheduler checks that

32
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:00,100
database, finds the tasks

33
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:00,927
that are ready,

34
00:01:00,947 --> 00:01:02,740
and hands them to the executor.

35
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:04,860
The executor sends them out to workers,

36
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,980
and the workers do the actual work.

37
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,860
Every result — queued,

38
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,580
running, success or failed

39
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:13,020
— goes back into the metadata database.

40
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:14,820
And the web UI sits on top,

41
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:16,540
so you can see every run,

42
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:17,380
read the logs,

43
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,153
and retry a failed task with one click.

44
00:01:20,173 --> 00:01:22,660
So when a team says, "we use Airflow,"

45
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:24,980
it means their pipelines are code.

46
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,140
They run on a schedule,

47
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:27,220
in the right order,

48
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:28,500
and when something breaks,

49
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,340
they know exactly where.

50
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,440
That's Apache Airflow.
